eaching sections of ENGR 104: Fundamentals of Engineering, CS Software, Code, and Security This course helps build foundational skills in structured problem solving and modeling that are broadly applicable across engineering and computer science fields. The instructor will play a critical role in helping students transition into college-level STEM coursework while promoting student engagement, resilience, and curiosity. Course delivery includes lectures, labs, group work, and projects contextualized by real-world engineering problems. Collaboration with faculty in engineering and computing-related fields is expected to ensure that course content reflects current and emerging industry expectations. Additional Responsibilities: • Develop AI training and ethical use related materials for all engineering students • Conduct regular office hours • Participate FEP planning and coordination meetings • Participate in college and university events • Serve on assigned college committees Minimum Qualifications The candidate must meet all the following required qualifications: • Master’s degree in Computer Science, Engineering Education, or a closely related field • Demonstrated ability to teach at the college level • Knowledge of algorithmic thinking and basic computational problem-solving • Excellent written and oral communication skills • Knowledgeable in AI and its use in the engineering classroom Preferred Qualifications • Prior experience teaching first-year engineering or computing students • Familiarity with MATLAB, Visual Basic, Python, or similar technical computing tools • Experience with interdisciplinary or project-based learning models • Commitment to STEM education Instructions and Required Materials for Application Application packages should consist of a cover letter, CV, a detailed statement of teaching philosophy and plans in addition to contact information for at least three references. About the Division/College/School As the premier flagship university for 