    "https://careers.ua.edu/jobs/freshman-engineering-program-teaching-assistant-professor-530368-tuscaloosa-alabama-united-states": {
      "title": "Freshman Engineering Program - Teaching Assistant Professor - 530368",
      "description": "Freshman Engineering Program - Teaching Assistant Professor - 530368 - Tuscaloosa, Alabama, United States My settings Featured Jobs Research Healthcare Information Technology (IT) Life at UA Campus Life Tuscaloosa Benefits Work Life Search Jobs Job Alerts Staff Jobs Student Jobs Faculty Jobs UA System Jobs My Application Kiosk mode Freshman Engineering Program - Teaching Assistant Professor - 530368 Tuscaloosa, Alabama, United States Apply Now Search by job title, location, department, category, etc. Search Freshman Engineering Program - Teaching Assistant Professor - 530368 Tuscaloosa, Alabama, United States Engineering Non-Tenure-Track/Clinical Faculty Closing at: Dec 31 2026 - 22:55 CST Department/Organization 214132 - Eng Student Services - Instruction Rank Teaching Assistant Professor Position Summary The Freshman Engineering Program and the Lee J Styslinger Jr. College of Engineering at The University of Alabama is seeking a qualified and enthusiastic renewable contract Teaching Assistant Professor to teach courses (ENGR 101 and ENGR 104) that support all engineering majors and serves as a gateway to success in the engineering curriculum as well as developing AI training for all engineering students. Detailed Position Information The Freshman Engineering Program and the Lee J Styslinger Jr. College of Engineering at The University of Alabama invite applications for full-time, nine month, renewable-contract Teaching Assistant Professor with focuses in computer science and AI. This position is non-tenure track, three-year appointment that is renewable at the discretion of the University. Primary responsibilities of this position include: Teaching sections of ENGR 101: The World of Engineering The focus of this course is to prepare freshman for successful study in engineering and computer science. The course concentrates on developing an understanding of one’s discipline, the contributions that one’s discipline makes to society, and the skills necessary for academic and career success in the field. Teaching sections of ENGR 104: Fundamentals of Engineering, CS Software, Code, and Security This course helps build foundational skills in structured problem solving and modeling that are broadly applicable across engineering and computer science fields. The instructor will play a critical role in helping students transition into college-level STEM coursework while promoting student engagement, resilience, and curiosity. Course delivery includes lectures, labs, group work, and projects contextualized by real-world engineering problems. Collaboration with faculty in engineering and computing-related fields is expected to ensure that course content reflects current and emerging industry expectations. Additional Responsibilities: • Develop AI training and ethical use related materials for all engineering students • Conduct regular office hours • Participate FEP planning and coordination meetings • Participate in college and university events • Serve on assigned college committees Minimum Qualifications The candidate must meet all the following required qualifications: • Earned PhD in Computer Science, Engineering Education, or a closely related field • Demonstrated ability to teach at the college level • Knowledge of algorithmic thinking and basic computational problem-solving • Excellent written and oral communication skills • Knowledgeable in AI and its use in the engineering classroom Preferred Qualifications • Prior experience teaching first-year engineering or computing students • Familiarity with MATLAB, Visual Basic, Python, or similar technical computing tools • Experience with interdisciplinary or project-based learning models • Commitment to STEM education Instructions and Required Materials for Application Application packages should consist of a cover letter, CV, a detailed statement of teaching philosophy and plans in addition to contact information for at least three references. About the Division/College/School As the premier flagship university for the state of Alabama and as one of the first five universities in the nation to offer engineering instruction, The University of Alabama (UA) has more than 175 years of proud engineering history. UA’s Lee J. Styslinger Jr. College of Engineering is committed to conducting cutting-edge research that advances the frontiers of science and engineering, translating such fundamental advancements through applied research and technology commercialization into impactful solutions which improve the human condition and address the challenges facing humankind, and using that immersive research environment to provide unparalleled educational experiences and opportunities for our students at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. As a College, we pride ourselves on educating and preparing the next generation of engineering and computer science scholars and professionals who will positively shape the future around us. Through our educational, research, and service activities that support our overall vision to be one of the most impactful engineering programs in the nation, our College continues to provide leadership in the many professional disciplines represented by our College and to the community, state, and nation as we advance our professions, the economy, and the lives of all those whom we serve. The College is comprised of seven departments: Aerospace Engineering and Mechanics; Chemical and Biological Engineering; Civil, Construction, and Environmental Engineering; Computer Science; Electrical and Computer Engineering; Mechanical Engineering; and Metallurgical and Materials Engineering. These departments currently deliver 14 different undergraduate and 17 different graduate degree programs. Overall, the College enrolls approximately 6,000 students, making it one of the largest colleges out of the more than 40,000 students on UA’s campus. While the university has been on a constant mission to advance in every aspect